g the relation master database. [Task 1]\n- Preserve four invariants: Store alone writes message facts; a conversation orders by canonical `sessionId` while `shardUid` is DB routing only; Store local transactions emit recoverable Reply/Delivery/Session results; `cmd=6` is `ORIGIN_CONNECTION_ONLY` and delivery cannot depend on the origin node surviving. For shared-hot-DB migration, enumerate old writer/new writer/switch/rollback writer per field; a shadow path records differences but never writes. [Task 1]\n- Conditional-pass blockers: client/server must confirm original-connection `cmd=6`, group receive command and ACK rules; DBA must approve session sequencing, durable idempotency and Result Outbox schema/index/retention; MQ/ops must confirm RabbitMQ topology, Confirm/Return/retry/DLQ, partition migration and high watermarks; lock a baseline after assigning existing uncommitted changes; define Snowflake workerId unique lease/renewal/collision stop before Access pre-generates IDs. [Task 1]\n- Physical sequence: first Store (Session Worker co-deployed), then Deliver/ACK, then core converges to Access, then Session/Push; Group/Relation migration is a separate data project with single-write authority, historical migration, Outbox catch-up, dual-read checks, API cutover, and old-entry shutdown. [Task 1]\n- Current limits to account for: ordinary group delivery has no transactional Delivery Outbox (`buildAndSendGroupDeliveryEvent` sends after commit), carries full `recipientUserIds`, uses 8 fixed partitions, writes every member's Session Index, and ACK retry scans Redis Pending Keys under one global lock. Capacity evidence remains contextual: acceptance redline is `cmd5==cmd6==cmd27==cmd7`, zero lost/duplicate/out_of_order/ackPending, and MQ/Result/Session Outboxes drained. [Task 1]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- Treating multi-device idempotency as `clientType` alone can collide for two devices of the same type -> lock a real `recipientEndpointKey` during